<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Experimental Details: Scaling Laws Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Typically, to evaluate fitted scaling laws, prior work has adopted the <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> metric, for it’s scale-invariant measure of a laws’ explanatory power over the observed data.
However, the choice of what data to hold-out as the test set can have a significant impact on the performance and it’s interpretation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
For this reason, and because multilingual scaling laws are used to measure more than just the predictive power of the law to larger scales, we use a set of different <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> metrics to measure different objectives.
These are:</p>
<ol id="A2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Hold-out a randomly selected <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the data points from the data. This gives an impression of the models’ ability to fit the variance, without measuring particular dimensions of fit.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> Hold-out the training runs with training tokens with the top <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> — Hold-out a couple of scales of model sizes, including the largest: <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=660M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>660</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=660M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8B</annotation></semantics></math> parameters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> Hold-out the largest compute parts of the training runs, where <math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=6ND" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=6ND</annotation></semantics></math> FLOPs. This will include mainly a combination of the larger model sizes, and longer training runs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> Hold-out all mixtures that are not monolingual, bilingual, or unimax. This allows the scaling law to get a baseline sense of each interacting language, but it has to infer the rest of the mixture scaling properties.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These metric variants allow us to unpack specifically where the scaling laws perform well, and for what purposes they are reliable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Experimental Details: Language Finetuning</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S6" title="6 Pretrain or Finetune? ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we experiment with continuous pretraining on a single language, starting from a massively multilingual model’s checkpoint.
For clarity, we refer to this as <em id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finetuning</em>, to distinguish it from pretraining from scratch.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by training massively multilingual models, using <span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Unimax</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2023</a>)</cite> language sampling across all <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="420" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>420</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">420</annotation></semantics></math> languages in MADLAD-400.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al. (2023)</a></cite> demonstrate this is an effective sampling strategy to perform well across languages, as is the objective with many large multilingual models.
The <span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Unimax</span> sampling rate per language is documented in <a href="#A2.T4" title="In Vocabulary Details ‣ B.2 Experiment Design ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>
Each of these models we train for <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, to reflect real-world practices.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using these Unimax checkpoints, across model scales, we then conduct continuous monolingual pretraining (which we call <em id="A2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finetuning</em> here) on all 48 languages separately in <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{eval}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>eval</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{eval}}</annotation></semantics></math> (48).
While finetuning, we also observe the loss across all <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{eval}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>eval</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{eval}}</annotation></semantics></math> (48)languages as well. We use the same hyperparameters as discussed for pretraining, but reset the learning schedule, and train for at least another <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30k+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30k+</annotation></semantics></math> steps.
These empirical results allow us to compare (across a range of <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,D,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,D,C</annotation></semantics></math>) the loss on language <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> between a model finetuned from a Unimax checkpoint, and a model pretrained monolingually from scratch on language <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Experimental Details: Language Transfer</h4>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4" title="4 How Do Languages Benefit or Interfere with Each Other? ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we measure how much training on <em id="A2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">source language</em> <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is beneficial for the test loss on the <em id="A2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target language</em> <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
A language transfer score tells a practitioner the extent to which training with each language would help or hinder their target language <math id="A2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are two ways in which we can measure this language transfer:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="A2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Bilingual Transfer Score.</span> This score measures how many training tokens a 50/50 (<math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>) bilingual model needs, relative to a monolingual target language <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> baseline, to reach the same validation loss <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A positive score indicates co-training with the source language has positive transfer, whereas a negative score indicates it hinders convergence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Finetuning Adaptation Score.</span> This score captures the effect on the target language’s loss <math id="A2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, when a massively multilingual model is finetuned only on the source language <math id="A2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.5.1 </span>Bilingual Transfer Score (BTS)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Bilingual Transfer Score asks how data-efficient a bilingual learner is, relative to a purely monolingual one, on some target language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
To estimate this, we pretrain two models, a monolingual one on <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and a bilingual one that samples tokens equally (50/50) from the source and target languages (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>).
We then measure how many more training tokens it takes the bilingual model to attain the same validation loss <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the monolingual model.
This “distance” between the learning curves is measured throughout training, at different reference horizons—or, number of tokens trained on.
In <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 How Do Languages Benefit or Interfere with Each Other? ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we use models with 2B parameters, and a reference horizon of <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!=\!42B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!=\!42B</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, as it roughly equates to <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> pretraining steps for our 2B parameter models, and learning curves have stabilized.
In <a href="#A3.F1" title="In C.2 Extended Results: Language Transfer ‣ Appendix C Extended Results ‣ Appendix ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> we measure how the BTS varies by model size, and by number of training tokens seen.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are able to compute the bilingual transfer scores, across every combination of pairings between 10 languages: English, Russian, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, Chinese, Vietnamese, Japanese, and Hindi.
These languages were chosen to give a distribution of high- and mid-resource languages from a variety of language families.
This totals 10 monolingual experiments (one for each language), and <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(10,2)=45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(10,2)=45</annotation></semantics></math> bilingual experiments, which provide the results for a 10x10 grid of Bilingual Transfer Scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In more detail, let <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{mono}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{mono}</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of tokens trained on language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by a monolingual model. This model attains test loss of <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(d_{mono})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(d_{mono})</annotation></semantics></math> after <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{mono}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{mono}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens on language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We record the number of training tokens <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{bi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{bi}</annotation></semantics></math> at which the bilingual model reaches the same loss, <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(d_{\text{bi}})=L_{t}(d_{mono})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(d_{\text{bi}})=L_{t}(d_{mono})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the <span id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bilingual Transfer Score</span> (BTS) as the signed step surplus</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BTS}_{s\!\rightarrow t}=-\frac{\sigma_{\text{bi}}(L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}}))-2d_{\mathrm{mono}}}{d_{\mathrm{mono}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>BTS</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.158em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BTS}_{s\!\rightarrow t}=-\frac{\sigma_{\text{bi}}(L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}}))-2d_{\mathrm{mono}}}{d_{\mathrm{mono}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{bi}}=\sigma_{\text{bi}}(L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{bi}}=\sigma_{\text{bi}}(L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}}))</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{bi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>bi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{bi}}</annotation></semantics></math> maps a loss value to the number of steps taken by the bilingual model to reach that loss.
In short, this score measures the relative training efficiency of the bilingual model compared to a monolingual model at reaching the same loss level for language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subtracting <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d_{\mathrm{mono}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d_{\mathrm{mono}}</annotation></semantics></math> and dividing by <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{mono}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{mono}}</annotation></semantics></math> simply centers the value on 0, and forces positive transfer to give a positive value.
A value of 0, implies <em id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">neutral</em> transfer, as the multilingual model requires exactly the same number of steps in the target language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as the monolingual model, a value below 0 implies <em id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">negative</em> transfer, as the multilingual model is slower to reach the loss <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{t}}(d_{\mathrm{mono}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and a value above 0 implies <em id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive</em> transfer, as the bilingual model reaches the target loss in <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;2d_{\mathrm{mono}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>mono</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2d_{\mathrm{mono}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that for <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 How Do Languages Benefit or Interfere with Each Other? ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> these scores are anchored on 2B parameter models, trained for <math id="A2.SS5.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!=\!42B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!=\!42B</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, for consistency.
But as model size increases, or tokens trained decreases, the language transfer scores improve monotonically.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.5.2 </span>Finetuning Adaptation Score (FAS)</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Complementary to BTS, the Finetuning Adaptation Score measures the <em id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instantaneous impact</em> of continued exposure to a single language on all others while holding model capacity fixed.
Specifically, it measures how finetuning a massively multilingual model on source language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> affects the performance of target language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because we only need to train on each source language once, and evaluate on all languages, it is less computationally expensive than BTS, which requires training on every pair of languages.
As such, we are able to derive <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38*38=1444" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>38</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>38</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1444</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38*38=1444</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>) comparisons from 38 finetuning experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start from a shared multilingual checkpoint: a Unimax pretrained model, trained for <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
For each source language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we continue pre-training (for simplicity we denote this as <em id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finetune</em>) exclusively on this language, and evaluate at regular intervals on every target language’s <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\!\in\!\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\!\in\!\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> validation set.
Let <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s\!\rightarrow\!t}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.158em" rspace="0.158em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s\!\rightarrow\!t}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> be the validation loss on <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> after <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> additional updates on <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{unimax}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{unimax}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the baseline loss of the shared Unimax checkpoint, before finetuning has begun.
The Finetuning Adaptation Score (FAS) aggregates the reduction in loss over a common time window <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,d_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,d_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FAS}_{s\!\rightarrow t}=\frac{1}{d_{\max}}\int_{0}^{d_{\max}}\!\bigl[L^{unimax}_{t}-L_{s\!\rightarrow\!t}(d)\bigr]\,\mathrm{d}d." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>FAS</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.158em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.158em" rspace="0.158em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FAS}_{s\!\rightarrow t}=\frac{1}{d_{\max}}\int_{0}^{d_{\max}}\!\bigl[L^{unimax}_{t}-L_{s\!\rightarrow\!t}(d)\bigr]\,\mathrm{d}d.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Positive values indicate that exposing the model to <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> accelerates learning or yields immediate gains on <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (“helpful transfer”), whereas negative values capture negative interference. Normalising by <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the score comparable across language pairs and training horizons.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because finetuning on language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> can cause unpredictable behavior in the validation loss of a target language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (it might immediately increase, or decrease then eventually increase), we start with deriving a series of features about the finetuning loss curve.
We calculate the following features for a finetuning loss curve:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A2.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline Loss Deviation (<math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s\rightarrow t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s\rightarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
For each language pair <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq t</annotation></semantics></math> we quantify the deviation in validation loss between the baseline <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{unimax}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{unimax}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the loss <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s\rightarrow t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s\rightarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math> on the final step:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s\rightarrow t}=L_{s\rightarrow t}(d_{\text{max}})-L^{unimax}_{t}(d_{\text{max}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s\rightarrow t}=L_{s\rightarrow t}(d_{\text{max}})-L^{unimax}_{t}(d_{\text{max}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I5.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s\rightarrow t}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s\rightarrow t}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> means fine–tuning on <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> helps <math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="A2.I5.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s\rightarrow t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s\rightarrow t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> means it hurts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptation Gain (<math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> This measures the area normalized reduction in loss on language <math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> from finetuning on language <math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows us to capture the relative learning dynamics of both the source and target language.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{l}=\frac{1}{d_{max}}\int_{0}^{d_{\text{max}}}\bigl[\,L^{unimax}_{l}-L_{s\rightarrow t}(d)\,\bigr]\;\mathrm{d}s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{l}=\frac{1}{d_{max}}\int_{0}^{d_{\text{max}}}\bigl[\,L^{unimax}_{l}-L_{s\rightarrow t}(d)\,\bigr]\;\mathrm{d}s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I5.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{l}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{l}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates net improvement; large values imply that <math id="A2.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>
benefits greatly from additional supervision.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each language transfer pair (<math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), we compose the following feature vector <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s\rightarrow t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s\rightarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As languages differ in intrinsic difficulty, we normalize each feature.</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{g}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{g}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{g_{l}-\mu_{g}}{\sigma_{g}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{g_{l}-\mu_{g}}{\sigma_{g}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\delta}_{s\rightarrow t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\delta}_{s\rightarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\delta_{s\rightarrow t}-\mu_{\delta,t}}{\sigma_{\delta,t}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\delta_{s\rightarrow t}-\mu_{\delta,t}}{\sigma_{\delta,t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">For adaptation gain, we use the global mean and standard deviation <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{g},\sigma_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{g},\sigma_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, and for baseline loss deviation we compute the mean and standard deviation <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{\delta,t},\sigma_{\delta,t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{\delta,t},\sigma_{\delta,t})</annotation></semantics></math> across all source languages <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> for each fixed target language <math id="A2.SS5.SSS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Estimating Bilingual Transfer Score (BTS)</h4>

<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the Bilingual Transfer Score experiments we empirically measured language transfer scores
<math id="A2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y}_{s\rightarrow t}^{\text{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>true</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y}_{s\rightarrow t}^{\text{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a subset
<math id="A2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathcal{L}\!\times\!\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>obs</mtext></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\text{obs}}\subset\mathcal{L}\!\times\!\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We construct a training set from these 90 experiments, using them as the gold labels.</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{s\rightarrow t}=\bigl[\tilde{g}_{s},\;\tilde{g}_{t},\;\tilde{g}_{s}-\tilde{g}_{t},\;\tilde{\delta}_{s\rightarrow t}\bigr]^{\!\top},\qquad y_{s\rightarrow t}=y^{\text{true}}_{s\rightarrow t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>true</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{s\rightarrow t}=\bigl[\tilde{g}_{s},\;\tilde{g}_{t},\;\tilde{g}_{s}-\tilde{g}_{t},\;\tilde{\delta}_{s\rightarrow t}\bigr]^{\!\top},\qquad y_{s\rightarrow t}=y^{\text{true}}_{s\rightarrow t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We fit a random-forest regressor <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{4}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{4}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> trees and unlimited depth.
The predictive performance is assessed by <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold cross-validation
(<math id="A2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\!=\!5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\!=\!5</annotation></semantics></math>), obtaining an <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}=0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}=0.85</annotation></semantics></math> and Spearman correlation of <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.88</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.88</annotation></semantics></math>.
After training on all observed pairs, <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is invoked on every unlabelled <math id="A2.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)\!\in\!\mathcal{L}\!\times\!\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)\!\in\!\mathcal{L}\!\times\!\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding predicted BTS values, that estimate the language transfer shown in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 How Do Languages Benefit or Interfere with Each Other? ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.7 </span>Experimental Details: Multilingual Capacity</h4>

<section id="A2.SS7.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.7.1 </span>Multilingual Capacity Scaling Laws</h5>

<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table B.5: </span><span id="A2.T5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The set of experiments designed to measure the <em id="A2.T5.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">curse of multilinguality</em>—or how language performance is impacted by both the number of training languages and the model size.</span>
We defined <span id="A2.T5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Version</span> mixtures with <span id="A2.T5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Num</span> languages, ranging between 4 and 50. For mixtures with 4 languages, we define a few different versions oriented to languages that might be more likely to be grouped together.
This set of experiments are summarized in the <span id="A2.T5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Capacity</span> row of <a href="#A2.T1" title="In Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ ATLAS : Adaptive Transfer Scaling Laws for Multilingual Pretraining, Finetuning, and Decoding the Curse of Multilinguality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</figcaption>
<div id="A2.T5.10" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:416.5pt;height:198.2pt;vertical-align:-96.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="A2.T5.10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Version</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Num</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Languages (alphabetical)</span></span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4v0</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">4</span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">de, es, fr, pt</span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4v1</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">4</span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">de, en, es, fr</span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4v2</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">4</span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">hi, ja, vi, zh</span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4v3</span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">4</span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">en, hi, pt, ru</span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.10.1.1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6v0</span></span>
